A||||45||88Style||3.4 DOHC questions||||1||
Z||000000||88Style||10-02-2001||02:28 PM||||Hello. I am kind of new to the board, and Fiero's for that matter but I have a question. I have just gotten ahold of a 1988 Fiero Formula. Sinse it has the good 5sp I thought about putting a 3.4lt DOHC V6. <P>From what I have read on many informative sites, the 3.4 pushrod motor is a direct replacement with the exception of relocating the starter. Is the 3.4 DOHC motor just as easy to install (ie will the tranny bolt up without an adapter, will the Fiero's computer run that engine or do I need the other one, ect). I have found the engine I want but I want to see how easy it is/is not.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||10-02-2001||03:25 PM||||First off, WELCOME!<P>Second, your partially correct. The DOHC will bolt to the existing tranny but other things will need changing or modification. It also uses a different computer. <P>The absolute way to get specific answers is to ask very specific questions of those who have been-there-done-that. If you'll notice in the upper right hand corner you'll see a search function. Click on that and search on DOHC or such. Then, start reading. You'll find answers to most all your general questions. If, after you've researched the conversion and you need more info, you may want to contact those on the list directly for answers. Most everyone here is very willing to help.<P>Again, welcome the the club!<P>------------------<BR>Roy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||10-02-2001||03:27 PM|||| Sorry, double post.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||10-02-2001||03:39 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/005018.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/005018.html</A> <P>Marc is selling his 3.4 TDC and all the info that goes with it.. hit him up for some of the info.. he got some form Terryk.. but he's got an engine that i'm sure he'd sell cheap... <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91||||reg||1
Z||000004||88Style||10-02-2001||03:44 PM||||Thank you for the warm welcome. I did see the search option and spent some good time reading up on it! But I still have some articles to read and some questions to ask.<P>Sinse I know I am going to have to use the 3.4 computer (per what was said above) I guess here are the questions I still have...<P>1. Can I use the Fiero accessories or do I need to pull them from the other car. <P>2. Will it bolt into the "engine cradle"?<P>3. Will the v6 5sp handle the power? (one of the articles said something about taking th transaxle out of the other car but I have also read that the later generation fwd 5sps won't work in a fiero)?<P>4. How hard is it to integrate another cars computer into a fiero (do you replace the fiero's computer or run it side by side)?<P>I'm sure I can think of more but I still have a lot to read and might find the other less pressing answers there.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88Style||10-02-2001||03:44 PM||||two posts...<p>[This message has been edited by 88Style (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DreXteR||10-02-2001||04:19 PM||||Welcome.<P>1. What do you mean by accesories?<BR>2. Yes with minor mods.<BR>3. As far I know yes.<BR>4. I beleive you have to use the other car ecm and do some rewire to fit the fiero other stuff.<P>Do a search, you will fond very interesting info. BTW msweldone have lotz of knowledge in this.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000007||88Style||10-02-2001||06:01 PM||||By accessories, I mean things like the AC compressor and alternator ect..<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||10-02-2001||06:24 PM||||1. It will bolt to the cradle.<BR>2. You need a custom A/C hose (Fiero chassis end and DOHC compressor end).<BR>3. You replace the Fiero ECM with the DOHC ECM completely. Consider this. The 94/95 ECM (6401) won't support manual trans. It will run fine, but you might have stalling problems during decel or shifting. The 91-93 (9396) supports manual trans and will work on a 94 and up engine. The 91-93 is not sequential (only 94 and up), but even the sequentials switch to batch fire above 2500 RPM. 91-93 is speed density (map), 94 and up is mass-airflow.<BR>4. Use the Fiero 5spd (282), the DOHC 5 spd (284) is too much BS to deal with.<BR>5. The DOHC will bolt to the Fiero 5 spd.<BR>6. You will need to fabricate a dogbone and the stock dogbone won't work. Consider adding the Fiero vibration dampener (mini-shock) too.<BR>7. You will lose the lid springs. Use a Rodney Dickman strut.<BR>8. If you use a 91-95 engine, you can get another front manifold, flip it over and run a Fiero style crossover. Otherwise the exhaust exists into the trunk and installing a cat and muffler is a b!tch. For 96 engines it's more difficult. I think the new heads and intake were 96.....<BR>9. They feel like a stock 2.8L below 2500-3000 and then come on like a mag dog above that.<BR>10. The intakes howl and can be annoying if you don't do something other than a K&N.<BR>11. The Fiero clutch is fine unless you plan on clutch drops. Then nothing is good. <BR>12. Put in good plugs because you can't change them without lowering the engine.<BR>13. Consider if smog testing is a problem in your area. <BR>14. It's not a weekend project. Assume it will take around 100 hours of work (by the time you are REALLY done, not just running). The ECM wiring is a big part of it.<BR>15. Figure an additional $1000 above the price of the engine.<BR>16. If you want torque for drag racing, don't use a DOHC. If you want to road race and be mean to other cars above 25 MPH, then a DOHC is excellent.<BR>17. Lord to they sound Indy!<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spektrum-87GT||10-02-2001||06:57 PM||||how much mechanical know how do you have? it can get quite expensive if you dont know what you're doing or think its an easy direct swap. The 3.4 pushrod is MUCH easier than the dohc, because most of the stuff is handled in the machine shop, its pretty much like putting in a 2.8 Another thing to remember, the dohc has only 10 more hp... is that worth all the extra money/work?||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||10-02-2001||07:08 PM||||I agree. In hindsight, the DOHC is *cool* but bang-for-the-buck, a nice 3.4L is an excellent choice. <P>My feeling is that unless you are committed to the swap, don't. Do a 3.2L or 3.4L.||||reg||14
Z||000011||terryk||10-02-2001||07:14 PM||||A 3.4L is 160HP, so the DOHC is 45 or 55 more HP (depending on the year). Once you get the 3.4L within 10, the DOHC price ain't so bad. <P>At the same time, the DOHC's torque is only slightly better (194 vs 205/210 ft-lbs or there 'bouts). And it shows.<P>But, at 7000 RPM.......||||reg||1
Z||000012||Spektrum-87GT||10-02-2001||08:47 PM||||i was wrong... according to <A HREF="http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm</A> <BR>the difference between the 3.4 pushrod is ~20hp, did you mean 45 or 55 more hp that the stock 2.8? just want to clear any confusion, because if there is a stock dohc 3.4 gm engine that makes 205-215hp i want to know about it!||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||10-02-2001||09:47 PM||||Oh, I see what you are saying. The later 3400 SFI's are around 180-ish, so you are right, about 25HP or so. Sorry, I didn't think about those. I meant the 3.4L Fiero-ized engines. The HT 3.4L upgrade is rated 160/194. I was talking about that engine.<P>I'd be curious as to how the 3400SFI performs against a 3.4L DOHC.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fierosound||10-02-2001||10:42 PM||||There's more work involved in installing a 3.4L DOHC<P>See this website  <A HREF="http://www.pelkie.com/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pelkie.com/fiero.htm</A> <P>||||reg||10
Z||000015||Spektrum-87GT||10-02-2001||11:14 PM||||ah, now i see what you are saying, didnt know gm switched from the 3.4 dohc to the 3400, now i feel like a doop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> i always thought they were the same engine.. so the 3.4 dohc made 210hp?||||reg||1
Z||000016||artherd||10-03-2001||09:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>because if there is a stock dohc 3.4 gm engine that makes 205-215hp i want to know about it!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>**91-93 3.4 DOHC (5sp) 210Hp 215lbs torque.<P>How does that grab you?<BR>(they sound *GREAT* when revved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  God I wish the Fiero came with this engine.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000017||88Style||10-03-2001||10:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierosound:<BR><B>There's more work involved in installing a 3.4L DOHC<P>See this website  <A HREF="http://www.pelkie.com/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pelkie.com/fiero.htm</A> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is a great article. Thanks so much for the information. I believe I have the mechanical abilities make the swap (I have built a prostreet 69 Nova when I was yonger and had two years of automechanics in highschool. What I can't do, I'm sure I can find help with some friends in the industry. My primary reasons for wanting the DOHC engine is...<P>1. 7k rpm stock<BR>2. Stock forged bottom end (from what I have read)<BR>3. The easy of addapting the engine to a forced induction system. <BR>4. Like mentioned before.. the sound!<BR>5. It seems to be one of the more rare modifications. Everyone seems to have northstar or small block chevy but I want to do something a little different. <BR>6. It's not going to be a critical project, I am willing to take all the time I need to do it and do it right. <BR>7. And lastly, I'm in it to win it but it's got to be fun!<P>Now that I've read and been up and down google.com looking for information, here is another set of questions I have..<P>1. What is the best engine/ecm combo for this modification?<BR>2. Can you reporgram a +96 ecm to work with the manual? (I'm guessing no but I can't find a binary on it).<BR>3. Is adapting the wiring harnesses just a matter of cutting and splicing wires and changing pinouts or is it more compicated. <BR>4. Is the only function of the Vehicle Speed Sensor to limit speed and is it a seperate unit from the speedo assembley? <BR>5. How do you adapt the AC hoses to work? <P>I couldn answer some of these questions by buying the book on the DOHC car but I don't know which one to buy so I can't buy a book. <P>It seems like this is a mechanicaly easy swap (no excessive cutting or welding). It seems the hard part is adapting everything to work together.. The loss of a deck lid spring doens't bother me so much. It would be so easy to adapt a strut from a hatch back car. <P>Thanks again to everyone for all the help and insight. I am getting really pumped about this project (for some odd reason, for all the cursing, blood, sweat, and tears, when a project is done, I really miss the cursing, blood, sweat, and tears).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Wannabe||10-03-2001||06:14 PM||||You should also have a look at this site to get more of a feel for the potential of this swap  <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||88Style||10-04-2001||10:32 AM||||Thanks again for all the information. I have read a ton about which engine to use but I can't seem to find a consensus. I have read that the 96+ flows much better, a good thing if you want to spin an already high rpm motor faster. I have also heard that the older ECM is the one to use with a manual transaxle. Is it possible to use the newer engine with the older ECM? What car should these componants come from?||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||10-04-2001||01:23 PM||||A concensus for what?<P>The swap is not easy, but it's not too bad. It just depends on your ability.<P>The 96+ engines are a bit better (larger intake tract), but I'm not sure they are *that* much better. This assumes you don't buy into the 282HP stock/tuned in the chip to save the trans rumor. I know Mike Smith and while he did get the engine way up in horse power, it's not very streetable. The torque didn't improve. <P>Any of the engines can be rewired to run on the 91-93 ECM.<P>For your questions:<P>1. The 16149396 ECM is the best for a manual trans combo. The best engine is the 96 and up, but I wouldn't turn down a 91-95 at all.<BR>2. No, I don't believe the 96 PCM has manual trans code in it, it's flash and it's not worth even attempting.<BR>3. The wiring is a matter of figuring out what wire goes where, routing it, cutting it, splicing it and dressing it.<P>You have to decide:<BR>1. Can the car be down for 100 hours of work.<BR>2. Can you afford $1000 over the cost of the engine for misc *stuff* and fabrication.<BR>3. Can you read service manual wiring diagrams and interpret the function of the wiring.<BR>4. Can you fabricate non-existant brackets and exhaust.<BR>4. Do you have several spare knuckles.<P>Several of us have done the swap. At the same time, none of us wrote it down either. Once you are done, writing a "how-to" is the last thing on your mind.||||reg||7
Z||000021||88Style||10-04-2001||01:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>A concensus for what?<P>The swap is not easy, but it's not too bad. It just depends on your ability.<P>The 96+ engines are a bit better (larger intake tract), but I'm not sure they are *that* much better. This assumes you don't buy into the 282HP stock/tuned in the chip to save the trans rumor. I know Mike Smith and while he did get the engine way up in horse power, it's not very streetable. The torque didn't improve. <P>Any of the engines can be rewired to run on the 91-93 ECM.<P>For your questions:<P>1. The 16149396 ECM is the best for a manual trans combo. The best engine is the 96 and up, but I wouldn't turn down a 91-95 at all.<BR>2. No, I don't believe the 96 PCM has manual trans code in it, it's flash and it's not worth even attempting.<BR>3. The wiring is a matter of figuring out what wire goes where, routing it, cutting it, splicing it and dressing it.<P>You have to decide:<BR>1. Can the car be down for 100 hours of work.<BR>2. Can you afford $1000 over the cost of the engine for misc *stuff* and fabrication.<BR>3. Can you read service manual wiring diagrams and interpret the function of the wiring.<BR>4. Can you fabricate non-existant brackets and exhaust.<BR>4. Do you have several spare knuckles.<P>Several of us have done the swap. At the same time, none of us wrote it down either. Once you are done, writing a "how-to" is the last thing on your mind.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The consensus I was looking for is on what the best motor is for it. The engine is going to be very modified. You brought up so good points about the swap that too many people don't consider when doing this type of work. We built a tube chassis car with a blown 468 (454 bored 30 over) that was based on a VW bug. Anything is possible if you lack sanity but can fill the void with money. The car is a toy so it being down for a while is only limited to how long my wife will tolerate the car and myself living in the garage. I can afford to do the swap and do it right or I wouldn't do it at all. I have seen and done to many 1/2 assed jobs when I was younger only to be dissappointed with the results because I didn't have the resources available. Exhaust fabrication is limited only to the number of u bends you can buy and how much gas/wire you have available. Changing a wiring harness is tedious work but it's not brain child stuff either if all you are doing is changing pins around. Changing the code and integrating systems together is where it becomes very hard. It doesn't appear that much coding is needed to do a straight swap. However, this engine will only run it's stock ECM for one year. My next years blow money on cars budget will allow an Electomotive EMS. Knuckles? Heh, if you don't have a lifetime supply, I would suggest another hobby! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks again for the information. This is going to be fun....||||reg||1
Z||000022||Spektrum-87GT||10-04-2001||06:09 PM||||if i do this swap, i promise to be the first to document every little detail and take pictures, maybe make a diagram of how the ecm is piggybacked to save a lot of time on future swaps. the forums have given a lot, and I'm sure will during the swap too, so after its over, time to give back. on a side note, i've heard that the dohc has 4 cams... is that true? must cost a crazy amount of $$$ to rebuild!||||reg||1
Z||000023||terryk||10-04-2001||07:11 PM||||Dual Over Head Cams. Originally called the  Twin Dual Cams (TDC). Formerly Melanie Haber. Audrey Farber? Susan Underhill? Betty-Jo Beoloski?<P>Oh, you mean Nancy!<P>Now that's an old joke!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Mach10||10-04-2001||10:49 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> Okie... I don't feel old anymore... What the HELL are you talking about, terryk?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000025||Formula88||10-04-2001||11:00 PM||||Yes, 4 cams.  Dual Over Head Cam.  2 Heads since it's a V6.  2 x 2 = 4.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>And yes, those cams are expensive from what I've heard.||||reg||4
Z||000026||lowCG||10-05-2001||12:45 AM||||The motor swap portion of my car was fairly straightforward;I did it in a garage in MI with hand tools in my spare time.It took about a month of measuring,wiring,parts gathering, then one evening it was in,one time,and a week later I was off to Seattle.<BR>The '97 motor(that's what the vin from the car produced)swapped in well,the later flash ecm works well after I purchased an air/fuel controller that allows instant changes,and added large resistors to take the place of the coils in the auto's shifter solenoids.<BR>The flywheel is a stock V6 unit with the counter weight ground off,stock clutch. <BR>The bracket that holds the shifter cables by the bellhousing needs to have a 1" standoff made to go under it for clearance.<BR>If you can get away from smog,the exhaust is just like a VW bug;manifold dumps in the center into two mufflers exiting out of the stock locations.<BR>The speedo just hooks up using the stock purple and yellow wires(they even match).<BR>The theft control system needs to be hacked around with,or just buy an electronic device from someone like I did.<BR>The motor mounts are just like stock,except that I used urethane and threw out the dogbone.<BR>The throttle cable is stock with a piece of tubing used as a drum on the end where it connects to the linkeage.<BR>The ECM mounts up in the passenger side wheelwell,I mounted a piece of angle to a web of metal up there.<BR>you need to punch another 3/4" hole in the firewall towards the driver's side from the other holes,and move both cables over.<BR>The radiator hoses fit like a dream,once I purchased a water neck from an S-10 and bolted it to the bottom of the stock 2.8 filler,and plugged the extra nipples.<BR>The hinges,as Terry mentioned,need to have their springs removed,and the pass. side hinge needs to have the lower half where it mounts to the firewall removed,leaving one bolt for attachment.<BR>These brakes I'm putting together now are making up for that smooth-going engine swap.<BR>Oh,and the wiring harness build-up does take about a week by itself,I tend to erase these not-as-fun portions of the project from my memory.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 10-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||terryk||10-05-2001||04:29 PM||||It (I) ain't that old!<P>||||reg||12
Z||000028||Spektrum-87GT||10-06-2001||01:36 AM||||i just dont understand how in the world it can have 4 cams, strange... i thought a 4 cylinder dohc engine setup was odd! can anyone else back that up? the 3.4 dohc has a total of 4 cams!?||||reg||1
Z||000029||terryk||10-06-2001||01:55 AM||||One intake and one exhaust cam on each head.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Standard||10-06-2001||04:47 PM||||Not too odd.. DOHC engines have been around for a looooong time.  I think the first DOHC 4 cyl was made sometime around the 1930's(?)  someone correct me if I'm wrong  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Formula88||10-06-2001||05:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>i just dont understand how in the world it can have 4 cams, strange... i thought a 4 cylinder dohc engine setup was odd! can anyone else back that up? the 3.4 dohc has a total of 4 cams!?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>On an inline 4 cylinder (Quad4) you have 2 cams.  Dual Overhead Cams on 1 head.  Since a V6 has 2 heads, and each head has dual overhead cams, that gives you 4 cams.  Make sense now?<P>Any V engine (3.4DOHC, Northstar, Aurora) that is DOHC has 4 cams.  ||||reg||3
Z||000032||DKOV||10-07-2001||04:56 AM||||It's kinda silly but...<P>Don't get the DOHC 3.4L and the TDC 3.4L confused.  They are NOT the same engine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I constantly hear them being intertwined as if they are the same name for the same engine but they are definately not the same engine.<P>Especially when you consider the 160 HP output of the DOHC with the 215 HP output of the TDC!<P>With mods and NO turbo, the TDC can easily put out around 300 HP if not 325!  You'll never get that out of a DOHC without any kind of boost.<P>Rip-your-head-off acceleration with NO turbo?  Yes it CAN be yours!<P>THIS... I know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000033||loafer87gt||10-07-2001||12:30 PM||||Umm, I'm 99.9% sure there wasn't any difference between the TDC and DOHC engines. Are you getting the OHV 3.4 pushrod based engine (160 hp) mixed up with the 3.4 DOHC? ||||reg||1
Z||000034||terryk||10-07-2001||12:40 PM||||DKOV, I think you are confused. The name on the TDC and DOHC changed before the engines did other than a few minor tweeks (cam sensor in the cam carrier, etc). In 96, they changed the intake and heads, but the bottom end is basically the same. Funny, for all of the work the HP didn't go up much either. <P>There was never a 160HP DOHC 3.4L. Well, from GM directly. Maybe another company made one.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Wannabe||10-07-2001||03:27 PM||||DOHC=Dual Over Head Cam a term used to describe any engine using that configuration<BR>TDC=Twin Dual Cam Nothing but a marketing term used to give GM DOHC V6 a tick sounding name, kind of like "Cross-fire Injection"||||reg||1
Z||000036||terryk||10-07-2001||03:33 PM||||There are two labels for the top of the DOHC engines.<P>Twin Dual Cam and Dual Overhead Cam. <P>I have both.||||reg||1
Z||000037||DKOV||10-07-2001||04:17 PM||||I'm not saying that I KNOW this to be true, but I am saying that my 91 GTP TDC is an entirely different motor than a DOHC... marketing or not.<P>Parts are not interchangable, listings are entirely different, etc.<P>The HP conversation keeps popping up because people keep saying that a DOHC 3.4 puts out around 160HP whereas I know that my 3.4 TDC came from the factory at 215.  I hear folks trying to get their 3.4L DOHC to a least make 200HP, but that is confusing if the 3.4LTDC is making 200+ bone stock...<P>They MUST be talking about two DIFFERENT motors because a little DOHC sticker can't rob THAT much power...<P>signed,<P>Thouroughly Confused||||reg||1
Z||000038||Neal||10-07-2001||05:18 PM||||You MUST be thinking the 3.4 OHV motor...<P>i also didnt think any version of the 3.4 existed till '93, but i may be wrong||||reg||1
Z||000039||DKOV||10-07-2001||06:30 PM||||Yessir...  I have a 1991 3.4L DTC mated to the factory 5-speed.  I guess they were very rare to begin with.  Even more rare with a 5-speed.<P>It came out of my beloved Grand Prix GTP that was totalled by a hit-n-run drunk driver.<P>In a way, it's automotive reincarnation now that it is installed in my Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000040||Mach10||10-07-2001||07:14 PM||||Aren't the GTPs the 3800 SC? ||||reg||5
Z||000041||Raydar||10-07-2001||10:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Dual Over Head Cams. Originally called the  Twin Dual Cams (TDC). Formerly Melanie Haber. Audrey Farber? Susan Underhill? Betty-Jo Beoloski?<P>Oh, you mean Nancy!<P>Now that's an old joke!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Por<I>gie!</I> Porgie Tirebiter!"<P>"Coming, Mother!"<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
